<Article slug="system-design">
<p>A catalogue displays a mug priced at £12. A seller changes its price to £15. Must every display update immediately? Must a price check used for a purchase consult the authoritative state?</p>
<p>Those are different requirements. For this example, a catalogue display may use a source observation for up to two seconds. An authoritative price check reads the source for that operation. We can now evaluate a cache design against two concrete promises, rather than asking whether it is simply “consistent”.</p>
<p>Follow an old read through the price change. Invalidation happens, yet the old reply arrives afterwards. The display can still use it briefly. Watch what changes when its original deadline passes.</p>
<ProductionCacheContract />
<p>At 1,500 ms, the display returns the old price while the authoritative check sees the new one. That difference is allowed by the display contract. At 2,100 ms, an old cached entry is unacceptable even if it remains physically present. When the source is unavailable too, the application refuses the answer.</p>
<p>This article assembles the collection’s mechanisms into a <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/redis-reference-app.zip" download>runnable reference application and load/failure lab</a>. It uses an HTTP catalogue API, SQLite as the authoritative source, and Redis as a disposable shared cache. The application is deliberately small enough to inspect. Its contracts and tests are the reusable part; its local credentials and numerical budgets are teaching inputs.</p>

<h2>Define the cache contract per operation</h2>
<p>A cache contract states which requests may share an answer, what makes that answer acceptable, how much work may be spent obtaining it, and what happens when the conditions cannot be met.</p>
<p>Our display endpoint accepts an observation younger than two seconds when it prepares the HTTP response. It does not promise the latest source value, immediate read-your-writes, or a limit on subsequent network transit and browser display time. The authoritative endpoint bypasses Redis, but a price observation alone is still not an entire purchase transaction. Stock reservation, final price agreement and payment require their own authoritative workflow.</p>
<p>For each operation, decide whether the system may return a previous observation, must validate a version, or must consult the source. An accepted age can be useful for display data. A permission check or inventory deduction may need a different path. A cache feature cannot choose the business guarantee for you.</p>
<p>The example chooses cache-aside with post-commit invalidation and an explicit age bound. It does not add L1, Cluster, a distributed lease or a durable invalidation stream by default. Each would create another boundary to implement and operate. Introduce one when a measured workload or stronger guarantee gives it a purpose.</p>

<h2>Cache keys, envelopes and validation</h2>
<p>Two tenants can have product 42, and one product can have English and Swedish display names. The cache key encodes a tuple containing the authenticated tenant, product ID and locale, under a versioned display namespace. The tenant comes from the application’s identity mapping, never from a freely chosen tenant query parameter.</p>
<p>The value is an <strong>envelope</strong>: a small object containing the schema version, the same request context, product data, source revision, observation time and validity deadline. The context in the key prevents normal collisions; checking it in the value also rejects an incorrectly written or restored object. The application checks both before accepting a hit.</p>
<p>The native lab warms alpha’s product 42, then reads beta’s product 42 and the Swedish representation. Their answers remain separate. It also writes malformed JSON, a different tenant, a different schema, an expired observation and an oversized value into the test cache. Each is rejected and replaced through an admitted source read.</p>
<p>Bound the bytes before decoding them. The example expects an envelope of at most 4,096 bytes and uses <code>GETRANGE key 0 4096</code>, retrieving at most 4,097 bytes. The extra byte detects an oversized value without fetching its entire contents. This choice fits small JSON strings; it is not a universal replacement for GET or other Redis data types.</p>
<p>A missing key, rejected envelope and failed Redis request are distinct outcomes. Keep them distinct in metrics even when they lead to the same fallback path. Do not let an invalid cached value become a successful hit merely because Redis returned it quickly.</p>
<ReferenceRequestPath />
<p>Choose “Redis unavailable, source full”. The path ends at admission refusal. Then choose “Source reply too old”. Obtaining a source result is still insufficient: the application refuses an observation which has exceeded its permitted age. There is no retry arrow which can circulate indefinitely.</p>

<h2>Preserve freshness through writes and delayed fills</h2>
<p>The database worker records the observation time when it reads the source row. The application computes <code>validUntil = observedAt + maxAge</code>. A delayed response carries that original deadline. Inserting it into Redis does not make the observation younger.</p>
<p>The Redis write uses only the remaining lifetime, and every cache read checks the deadline in the envelope. Both matter. A SET can be delayed after its remaining TTL was calculated; a restored entry can have an inappropriate physical lifetime. The payload check limits what the application accepts even when Redis has not removed the key yet.</p>
<p>The final response path checks age again. A value which was acceptable before a long wait can become unacceptable before it is returned. The reference application assumes its worker and application share an aligned host clock. Across hosts, define the tolerated clock error and clock-adjustment behaviour as part of the guarantee. A timestamp is not automatically a distributed-clock proof.</p>
<p>On a write, the source changes first. The application then attempts to delete both locale entries. This usually removes an old answer sooner, but it is not the sole protection against indefinite staleness. A missed DEL or a late fill can leave an old value; its original age limit still applies.</p>
<p>The lab forces exactly that race. A source read observes 1,200 pence and pauses. A conditional update commits 1,500 pence, then deletes the Redis entries. The old reply is released and fills Redis afterwards. A display read can return 1,200 within its original budget; the authoritative endpoint returns 1,500. After the deadline, the display must reload or refuse.</p>
<p>For speed, that native exercise uses a 500 ms age budget; the interactive application defaults to two seconds. Neither is a benchmark or a generally correct TTL. A second test holds the source reply beyond its own age budget and verifies that the application neither returns nor caches it.</p>
<p>If the product instead requires immediate read-your-writes, this design is insufficient. Use an authoritative path or an explicit version-validation protocol with the failure behaviour required by that promise. If invalidation must eventually reach several dependent caches, a durable change mechanism may be needed. The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/invalidation/">invalidation article</a> explains those races and choices. Adding a DEL to a write handler does not establish the stronger guarantee.</p>

<h2>Bound waiting and unfinished work separately</h2>
<p>A caller deadline says how long a caller will wait. It does not prove the underlying work stopped. If every timeout frees a permit while its database operation continues, the application can exceed its intended source limit precisely when the source is slow.</p>
<p>The example therefore keeps a source permit until the worker replies, acknowledges cancellation or exits. SQLite runs in a separate worker so a blocking query cannot freeze the HTTP event loop. The worker may still be unable to interrupt a synchronous operation immediately; its permit remains occupied during that interval.</p>
<p>The native test deliberately blocks the worker. The HTTP request receives an unavailable response when its source wait expires, but the source-active count stays at one. With a limit of one, another load is refused until the worker actually finishes. A separate test verifies that an unfinished HTTP handler also remains counted after its waiting caller times out.</p>
<p>This accounting is useful even with a cancellable database driver. Verify when cancellation is acknowledged and what it means for already committed work. Our update uses an atomic conditional SQLite statement with an expected revision. A timeout can still leave the client uncertain whether a write committed; the application does not automatically retry it. Reconcile authoritative state and revision before deciding on another write.</p>
<p>Redis has the same distinction between waiting and execution. The earlier <a href="/learn/databases/caching/clients-and-latency/">client lab</a> showed that the pinned node-redis 6.3.0 queue timeout does not bound an already-sent command. This application uses an external wait deadline and discards its owned connection on timeout. Pending client state is bounded; offline queuing and automatic command replay are disabled.</p>
<p>Discarding a connection may fail other pending operations, and a sent command may already have executed. The cache adapter performs no retry loop within the request. A failed fill is optional; a delayed fill retains the payload’s original deadline. A failed invalidation is counted, and the chosen age contract still applies.</p>
<p>Recovery permits one connection attempt at a time after a cooldown. Requests which cannot use the cache still face source admission. The lab pauses its own Redis process, observes the external deadline and source fallback, then verifies recovery. It also stops and restarts Redis entirely. These are controlled failure exercises, not measurements of production failover time.</p>

<h2>Coalescing, admission control and load shedding</h2>
<p>Forty misses for one key are different from forty misses for forty keys. The first group can share a source read. The second group needs independent work or refusals. Coalescing reduces duplicate work; admission control limits the work which remains.</p>
<p>The application allows at most sixteen handlers to wait for one shared fill, including its leader. It separately limits source concurrency and the rate of starting work. The next illustration holds the source replies until the entire burst has arrived. Switch between one key and distinct keys, then complete the reads.</p>
<CacheBurst />
<p>For the shared key, one source read serves sixteen accepted requests and twenty-four are refused. With distinct keys, four source reads are admitted and thirty-six requests are refused. The runnable lab asserts those outcomes through actual HTTP while Redis is stopped.</p>
<p>A waiter limit is necessary even when only one source query runs: waiting requests still occupy memory, sockets and application state. The example also bounds unfinished HTTP handlers, connections, request bodies and Redis pending operations. These limits protect different resources; none replaces all the others.</p>
<p>The source uses a token bucket as well as a concurrency limit. Its interactive defaults are four active operations, twenty tokens replenished per second and an initial burst of four. Twenty tokens per second is not a hard maximum of twenty starts in every one-second interval: the available burst can be spent too. The deterministic burst tests freeze refill at zero so they can isolate the four initial tokens.</p>
<p>A refused request receives 503 rather than joining an unbounded queue. A real service must define what callers do next. Immediate retries can recreate the overload; the caller’s deadline, retry policy and user experience belong to the same design. Test the source-failure path as deliberately as the normal hit path.</p>

<h2>Allocate budgets across the whole fleet</h2>
<p>Four source permits in each application process do not mean four source operations across the service. Scaling from one process to four can create sixteen concurrent loads, even though every process obeys its own limit.</p>
<FleetAdmission />
<p>At four processes, the unpartitioned settings allocate sixteen permits and eighty starts per second of token refill, exceeding the assumed fleet budgets. Dividing the budget gives each process three permits, fifteen starts per second and a burst of three.</p>
<p>Static partitions are simple but can waste capacity when one process is busy and another is idle. A shared admission mechanism can coordinate capacity, but then its availability and failure policy matter. Autoscaling and rolling deployments must not temporarily give every new instance the old fleet’s full allocation.</p>
<p>The reference application supplies local bounds, not a distributed source-capacity guarantee. Before deploying an adapted design, allocate the database budget across all callers, including background jobs and services which bypass this cache.</p>

<h2>Run the application and test the contract</h2>
<p>Download the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/redis-reference-app.zip" download>reference application</a>, install Node 24.16 or later in the 24.x line and an official Redis 8.2.2 binary, then follow its README. <code>npm start</code> creates a private Redis process, a temporary SQLite database and a loopback HTTP API. <code>npm test</code> runs the acceptance and load/failure exercises. The README includes complete requests and explains how to select the Redis binary.</p>
<p>Two display reads expose the cold and warm paths; the warm read leaves the source-read count unchanged. The price-check endpoint bypasses Redis. A conditional PATCH updates the source before invalidation. Aggregate metrics expose hits, rejected entries, cache errors, source starts, refusals and active work. The demo credentials are public examples, and the server is intentionally local. Replace authentication, secret distribution, network and TLS policy before adapting it into a deployed service.</p>
<p>The tests are executable statements of the design: tenant and locale separation; invalid-entry rejection; stale refill within a preserved deadline; refusal beyond that deadline; bounded work during Redis outage; cancellation accounting; and recovery from an empty cache while the source retains the updated price. They use explicit barriers and injected delays. Their result is whether the contract holds under those schedules, not an advertised throughput figure.</p>
<p>The runner owns its processes, sockets and temporary data and cleans them up on completion or handled interruption. Ending the run removes the demo source database too. Its Redis restart test preserves SQLite only within that run; it is not a database disaster-recovery test. Node’s SQLite interface is a release candidate in the tested baseline, and the worker exists to keep its synchronous calls outside the HTTP event loop.</p>

<h2>Choose the next mechanism from evidence</h2>
<p>Once the simple contract works, use workload evidence to decide what to change. Low reuse may mean caching is not worthwhile. Repeated reads of a hot shared value may justify L1, with the additional invalidation and memory boundaries from <a href="/learn/databases/caching/client-side-caching/">client-side caching</a>. Independent work exceeding one node’s capacity may justify <a href="/learn/databases/caching/redis-cluster/">Redis Cluster</a>; one indivisible hot key needs a different remedy.</p>
<p>Persistence and replicas can reduce recovery cost, but do not establish the application’s freshness guarantee. A larger cache may reduce misses, but cannot compensate for unsafe key equivalence. A distributed lease may reduce duplicate fills, but does not make a stale lease holder authoritative. Keep each mechanism attached to the problem it actually solves.</p>
<p>Before a release, rehearse the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/operations/">operational runbook</a> with the actual client, credentials, topology and source budget. Verify a cold start, a source update racing a fill, a missed invalidation, a delayed Redis reply, a source failure and a recovery rollout. State the expected answer or refusal before running each case.</p>
<p>A successful cache design gives the application useful reuse while preserving explicit limits on age, work and failure. The most valuable result of this collection is the ability to explain those limits, demonstrate them under an adverse schedule, and recognise when an operation requires a different contract.</p>

